{
  "coreName": "PCSX2",
  "systemId": "PS2",
  "author": "the PCSX2 Dev Team",
  "url": "https://github.com/PCSX2/pcsx2",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "_machines_note": "ONE core.wbx, four machines. PCSX2 is a PlayStation 2, and NAMCO's System 246, System 256 and Super System 256 are PlayStation 2s: a Sony COH-H arcade motherboard with a NAMCO board bolted into the DEV9 expansion bay - a JVS I/O board where the controller ports would be, an ATA/ATAPI drive for the game's media, a serial link to a drive board, 32KB of settings memory and up to 128MB of board RAM. The EE, the IOP, both vector units, the GS, the software renderer, the SPU2, the savestate path and the disc readers are the same silicon and the same code; what differs is the board, the panel, the files a project takes, and the clock. A System 246 runs the EE at the console's 294.912 MHz, a System 256 a third faster and a Super 256 a half faster, which is the whole difference between those two. Which machine a session is comes from the machine setting, so a project pins it and a movie cites it; a movie made on one does not run on another. The bios is the discriminator a person cannot see: a COH-H rom and a retail rom are both 'a PS2 bios' and neither will boot the other's software.",
  "machineSetting": "machine",
