  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    64,
    16,
    3072
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A PlayStation 2 is the biggest machine in the bundle, and most of the mmap arena is not the console: 285MB is the machine (32MB of EE RAM, the bios and rom areas, the IOP's 2MB, the vector units, and the vtlb's two page tables over a 4GB address space) and 305MB is the recompiler cache region this build reserves and never writes. The sandbox charges for pages that are TOUCHED, so most of that is address space rather than memory - but the arena has to be big enough to hold it. The mmap arena is the one that matters and the one that grew: musl sends every LARGE allocation to mmap rather than to the heap, so the machine's memory, the code region and - when the renderer setting asks for OpenGL - a whole Mesa all come out of it. Growing sbrk instead does nothing, and taking from mmap to pay for it makes startup fail earlier, which is how this was found. The whole layout has to fit inside a single 4 GiB region.",
  "video": {
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded.",
    "drawEveryFrame": true,
    "_comment_drawEveryFrame": "Never tell this core to stop drawing; turbo skips only the readback. Turbo's bet is that a skipped frame is work deferred - but this renderer's picture lives on the far side of the GPU bridge and PERSISTS there, so a screen the game paints once and then leaves alone is painted by exactly one frame, and skipping that frame loses it for good. A warm-up cannot fix that: measured on Gran Turismo 4, drawing the last ten frames before a seek's destination still leaves 1.3% to 2.8% of the picture wrong at every point tried, while drawing throughout is exact. It costs almost nothing - 1500 frames on a GTX 1060: 8.28s turbo, 8.30s drawing, 9.75s drawing AND reading back - because what turbo was really saving is the readback, which this still skips. Both renderers need it: this is the display stage, not the GPU.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the display circuits the game programs. 640x448 is the ordinary NTSC frame, 1280x1024 the most the GS scans out.",
    "width": 1280,
    "height": 1024,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 448,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "SPU2 mixes at 48kHz, so a frame is about 800 stereo samples. The time stretcher upstream turns on by default is OFF here: a frame's samples are the ones the chip produced, and buffering is the frontend's business.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".iso": "PS2",
    ".bin": "PS2",
    ".img": "PS2",
    ".chd": "PS2",
    ".cso": "PS2",
    ".zso": "PS2",
