  "settings": [
    {
      "name": "machine",
      "display": "Machine",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "ps2",
        "system246",
        "system256",
        "system256super"
      ],
      "default": "ps2",
      "description": "Which of this package's machines this project is. 'ps2' is the PlayStation 2 console. The other three are NAMCO's arcade boards, which ARE PlayStation 2s - a Sony COH-H motherboard with a NAMCO board in the expansion bay - and they take a different kind of project: an arcade bios from a COH-H board rather than a retail one, the game's security dongle, its media and a boot program, each in its own slot, and a JVS panel in place of the controller ports. 'system246' runs the EE at the console's 294.912 MHz, 'system256' a third faster (393.216 MHz) and 'system256super' a half faster (442.368 MHz) - the clock is the difference between those two boards, and a game recorded on one does not replay on the other. A machine is the whole project: its inputs, its files, its settings and its firmware follow from it, and a movie made on one does not run on another."
    },
    {
      "name": "port1",
      "display": "Controller 1 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2",
        "guitar",
        "jogcon",
        "negcon",
        "popn",
        "guncon2"
      ],
      "default": "dualshock2",
      "description": "What port 1 holds. 'dualshock2' is the standard pad, and what all but a handful of games expect. The instruments are for the games built around them: 'guitar' is the Guitar Hero controller (a strum bar, five frets, a whammy bar and a tilt sensor), 'jogcon' is Ridge Racer's force-feedback dial, 'negcon' is the twisting Namco pad, and 'popn' is Pop'n Music's nine buttons. They are offered on the two physical ports only.\n\n'guncon2' is Namco's light gun, for Time Crisis and the dozen or so games like it. It is not a controller: it plugs into the console's USB socket of this same number, so choosing it leaves the CONTROLLER socket empty - which is what a real console with a light gun on it looks like. The number matters as well as the choice, because a few games only look for the gun on USB port 2. Where the gun is pointing arrives as two ordinary axes in the movie, so a recording replays anywhere; nothing here reads the mouse.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks what is connected, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port2",
      "display": "Controller 2 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2",
        "guitar",
        "jogcon",
        "negcon",
        "popn",
        "guncon2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What port 2 holds. 'dualshock2' is the standard pad, and what all but a handful of games expect. The instruments are for the games built around them: 'guitar' is the Guitar Hero controller (a strum bar, five frets, a whammy bar and a tilt sensor), 'jogcon' is Ridge Racer's force-feedback dial, 'negcon' is the twisting Namco pad, and 'popn' is Pop'n Music's nine buttons. They are offered on the two physical ports only.\n\n'guncon2' is Namco's light gun, for Time Crisis and the dozen or so games like it. It is not a controller: it plugs into the console's USB socket of this same number, so choosing it leaves the CONTROLLER socket empty - which is what a real console with a light gun on it looks like. The number matters as well as the choice, because a few games only look for the gun on USB port 2. Where the gun is pointing arrives as two ordinary axes in the movie, so a recording replays anywhere; nothing here reads the mouse.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks what is connected, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port3",
      "display": "Controller 3 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 1's multitap slot B, which needs multitap1 on. 'dualshock2' is the standard pad, and what all but a handful of games expect.\n\nA slot's device is part of the machine: a game asks the SIO bus what is connected, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port4",
      "display": "Controller 4 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 1's multitap slot C, which needs multitap1 on. 'dualshock2' is the standard pad, and what all but a handful of games expect.\n\nA slot's device is part of the machine: a game asks the SIO bus what is connected, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
