        "ps2-0170a-20030325",
        "ps2-0170e-20030227",
        "ps2-0170j-20030206",
        "ps2-0190a-20030623",
        "ps2-0190c-20030623",
        "ps2-0190e-20030623",
        "ps2-0190h-20030623",
        "ps2-0190r-20030623",
        "ps2-0200a-20040614",
        "ps2-0200e-20040614",
        "ps2-0200h-20040614",
        "ps2-0200j-20040614",
        "ps2-0210j-20040917",
        "ps2-0220a-20050620",
        "ps2-0220a-20060210",
        "ps2-0220a-20060905",
        "ps2-0220e-20050620",
        "ps2-0220e-20060210",
        "ps2-0220e-20060905",
        "ps2-0220h-20060210",
        "ps2-0220h-20060905",
        "ps2-0230a-20080220",
        "ps2-0230e-20080220",
        "ps2-0101jd-20000217",
        "ps2-0101xd-20000224",
        "ps2-0110ad-20000727",
        "ps2-0120ed-20000902",
        "ps2-0150ad-20001228",
        "ps2-0150jd-20010118",
        "ps2-0170ad-20030325",
        "ps2-0170ed-20030227",
        "ps2-0180cd-20030224",
        "ps2-0180j-20031028",
        "ps2-0250j-20100415",
        "ps2-dvd-110u",
        "ps2-dvd-120e",
        "ps2-dvd-120u",
        "ps2-dvd-130e",
        "ps2-dvd-130u",
        "ps2-dvd-200j",
        "ps2-dvd-210e",
        "ps2-dvd-210j",
        "ps2-dvd-210u",
        "ps2-dvd-212g",
        "ps2-dvd-212u",
        "ps2-dvd-213e",
        "ps2-dvd-215g",
        "ps2-dvd-300e",
        "ps2-dvd-300u",
        "ps2-dvd-302c",
        "ps2-dvd-302e",
        "ps2-dvd-302u",
        "ps2-dvd-310",
        "ps2-dvd-311"
      ],
      "default": "ps2-0230a-20080220",
      "description": "WHICH PlayStation 2 this project is. A PS2 cannot run a single instruction without its bios - there is no high-level reimplementation to fall back on - and which one it runs decides the console's region, its language, its menus, and what a game checks when it refuses to run. Each choice expects one exact dump: the firmware page names the file and the SHA1 it wants. Two projects on different bios are different machines and do not share movies. The retail consoles come first; after them the DEVKIT and debug-station roms, and last the DVD PLAYER firmware, which is the disc player's own rom rather than a boot rom - a machine will not start from one.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "bios",
      "display": "Boot ROM",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "coh-h-246c",
        "coh-h-256",
        "coh-h-256-mirrored",
        "coh-h-a000010"
      ],
      "default": "coh-h-246c",
      "description": "WHICH arcade board this project is, in the only way the hardware can be told apart. Sony never moved the version or the date on a COH-H rom - every one of them says v1.00 (2000-03-06) - so the EXTINFO build serial inside the dump is the discriminator, and it is what names the board: 20021119-163841 is a System 246 Rack C, 20040519-145634 a System 256 (the Super 256 runs the same rom with the bus overclocked), 20000901-114731 the COH-H A-000-010 development board. 'coh-h-256-mirrored' is the same System 256 rom dumped at 4 MB with its two megabytes written twice, which is what some dumpers produce; it is offered because it is a real dump in circulation and this core pins firmware by hash. A RETAIL PlayStation 2 bios will not do and is not offered here: the arcade boot software differs across a plethora of module interfaces and an arcade game will not run on it. The firmware page names the file and the SHA1 it wants.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "arcade_game",
      "display": "Game ID",
      "type": "string",
      "default": "",
      "description": "NAMCO's part number for this game - \"NM\" and five digits, NM00004 for Tekken 4, NM00007 for Soul Calibur II, NM00019 for Tekken 5. It is the game's IDENTITY the way a MAME set name is, and it is what the JVS board reads to know how this cabinet's panel was wired: which switch bit is which face button, whether there is a wheel or a drum or a pair of levers, where a light gun's sensor reports. A project that leaves it empty gets the board's generic wiring, which boots and is playable and is not the cabinet the game was built for.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "arcade_media",
      "display": "Media",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "cd",
        "dvd",
        "hdd"
      ],
      "default": "dvd",
      "description": "What kind of medium the game shipped on, which decides how the board's own ATA/ATAPI controller reads it. A COH-H board has no laser of its own, so the media is in the NAMCO board's drive and not in the console's tray - except for a CD title, which the game also asks the console for, and which this core therefore mounts in both places. Stated rather than guessed: the fork this board comes from looks it up in a game database, and a project states its own settings.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "arcade_ram",
      "display": "Board RAM",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "0",
        "32",
        "64",
        "128"
      ],
      "default": "64",
      "description": "How much RAM the rack carries on the NAMCO board, in megabytes. A System 246 Rack A takes expansion PCBs of 32 or 64MB in two slots; Racks B and C have 64MB built in; Wangan Midnight's cabinet carries 128; a System 256 has none at all, and should be set to 0. It is a setting rather than something guessed from the game, because the board RAM is part of every savestate this core takes and 128MB of it is not free - and because a cabinet is a thing somebody built, which a project has to be able to state. A game that reaches for RAM the rack has not got reads zero, which is what an empty expansion slot does.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "arcade_panel",
      "display": "Panel",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "derived",
        "standard",
        "fighting",
        "lightgun",
        "racing",
        "drum",
        "twinstick",
        "touch"
      ],
      "default": "derived",
      "description": "How the cabinet's JVS loom is wired. 'derived' takes it from the Game ID, which is right for every game the board knows and is what a project should normally leave it at; the rest force a layout, for a game the table has not got or a cabinet somebody converted. The choice decides which of the Arcade Panel's controls the board actually reads - a fighting cabinet reads six switches and no analog, a racing one reads the wheel and the pedals, a Taiko cabinet reads eight drum sensors - and it is machine state, so a movie cites it.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "arcade_region",
      "display": "iLink Signature",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "asia4",
        "asia5",
        "japan"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "The regional signature a System 256 reads out of the board's own iLink id, which is how the Taiko Drum Master games decide which region they are in. 'none' leaves the id the machine's bios carries. It does nothing on a System 246.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dip_test",
      "display": "DIP 1: Test Mode",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "The I/O board's test switch, as it is on the real board: a DIP SWITCH rather than a button, which is why it is a setting and not a column in the movie. On, the cabinet boots into its own test menu instead of the game - where the coin settings, the input test and the game's own options live. Whatever the switch is set to is part of the machine a movie was recorded on.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dip_video_voltage",
      "display": "DIP 2: Video Voltage",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "The second of the I/O board's four DIP switches, reported to the game as part of the board's status word. Like the other three it is machine state a movie has to cite, and the default is the position a cabinet leaves the factory in.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dip_monitor_sync",
      "display": "DIP 3: Monitor Sync Frequency",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "The third DIP switch: which sync frequency the cabinet's monitor runs at, 15kHz or 31kHz. Reported to the game, which some titles read to pick a video mode.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dip_video_sync_split",
      "display": "DIP 4: Video Sync Split",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "The fourth DIP switch: whether the cabinet's monitor takes composite sync or separate horizontal and vertical. Reported to the game as part of the same status word.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "in": [
          "system246",
          "system256",
          "system256super"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
